"tpot": 0.015278701221241671, "text": "1.  **Deconstruct the User's Request**:\n    *   Topic: Write-ahead log (WAL) and database crash consistency.\n    *   Specific points to cover: fsync ordering, checkpoints, recovery process.\n    *   Length constraint: ~300 words.\n\n2.  **Initial Brainstorming & Structuring**:\n    *   *Introduction*: Define WAL and its core principle (write log first, then data).\n    *   *fsync ordering*: Explain the strict ordering requirement (log fsync before data page write/fsync). Why? To ensure the log has the record of the change before the data file is modified.\n    *   *Checkpoints*: What are they? Periodically flushing dirty pages to disk and truncating the log. Why? To bound recovery time and log size.\n    *   *Recovery*: What happens on crash? Scan log, redo (apply) committed transactions, undo (rollback) uncommitted transactions. Use the log's LSNs (Log Sequence Numbers) to determine what was applied.\n    *   *Conclusion*: Summarize the guarantee (atomicity and durability).\n\n3.  **Drafting the Content (Mental or scratch", "worker": 0, "label": "prof-greedy"}
